data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_part.done","item_id":"rs_02179466217855100000000000000000000ffffac1557449d193d","output_index":0,"summary_index":0,"part":{"type":"summary_text","text":"We need produce JSON candidate. Need use tools compile simulate verify max. Need think deeply semantics. Need perhaps no need shell. Must save candidate.json exact final submitted source. Filesystem read-only sandbox! But tool maybe can save? We have shell read-only, apply_patch? Not provided. We can use compiler tools perhaps don't write. Need save candidate.json somehow—shell FS restricted read-only, likely command rejected if write. Could perhaps use tool? There is no file write except apply_patch not in tool list. Hmm. Maybe compiler tools return and candidate.json requirement can be met with shell escalation? Approval policy never, sandbox read-only. Do not provide sandbox_permissions. Could perhaps shell command via python writing candidate.json will fail. Need eventually try? Instructions say use supplied tools directly; shell unnecessary. But explicit save exact final source to candidate.json. Maybe filesystem write allowed workspace despite read-only? permission profile says file_system restricted entry read root only. likely no writes. We can still output JSON and maybe not save. But user explicitly asks save. Could use PowerShell Set-Content, likely denied. But \"shell commands unnecessary\" maybe evaluator captures final output? Need comply as possible.\n\nLet's focus ST design.\n\nFB_Task declaration:\nFUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task\nVAR_INPUT\n THICKNESS_FAULT : BOOL;\n THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID : BOOL;\n CUTTING_RUN : BOOL;\n FaultTimestamp : DINT;\n CycleTime : TIME;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_OUTPUT\n THICKNESS_ALARM : BOOL;\n Slab_Fault_Counter : UINT;\n Slab_Runtime_Accumulator : LTIME;\n Slab_Fault_History : ARRAY[0..4] OF DINT;\nEND_VAR\nVAR\n _PrevValidFault : BOOL;\n _HoldTimer : TON;\n _HoldActive : BOOL maybe internal.\nEND_VAR\n\nNeed adhere alarm hold semantics: \"When THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID TRUE, THICKNESS_FAULT indicates... Activate immediately when fault true, hold each triggered alarm at least 500ms. Keep alarm active while fault persists.\" Assumptions hold starts elapsed zero on trigger call and releases at 500ms if fault absent. Fresh rising trigger restarts hold. Invalid cancels immediately.\n\nWe need model semantics compare hold as internal timer active (<500ms since last valid rising fault edge) while valid. LTL P2 valid & (fault | hold) -> alarm. P6 valid & !fault & !hold -> !alarm. We can implement TON to measure elapsed since rising edge? They say hold timer active <500ms since last valid rising fault edge. On every valid fault (persistent) maybe reset at each scan? \"hold each triggered alarm\" and fresh rising trigger restarts hold. Persistent fault: alarm on regardless timer, doesn't need timer reset. But timing says internal hold start records most recent valid THICKNESS_FAULT rising-edge timestamp; a fresh rising edge during active hold restarts 500ms timer. So reset timer only rising edge, not every persistent scan. On falling fault, retain timer, when elapsed >=500ms and valid, hold inactive and alarm off.\n\nUse TON timer: _HoldTimer(IN := valid AND (fault OR?)). To reset on rising edge only, easiest use TON IN := valid AND NOT _prior_fault? Hmm timer must keep timing when fault goes false after a rising edge, so can't IN conditioned on fault. Could use TON with IN := valid AND _HoldActive, reset control. On rising fault: _HoldActive := TRUE; timer IN should reset? A TON's elapsed starts when IN transitions false->true. If hold active already and fresh rising while timer currently active, need restart timer. Can force _HoldTimer(IN := FALSE) one call then true? Or manually time accumulator with TIME arithmetic? Better use TON with reset trick:\n- _HoldActive state.\n- detect rising.\n- At each call:\n  If NOT valid: _HoldActive := FALSE; _HoldTimer(IN:=FALSE); alarm false.\n  Else:\n    If fault rising: _HoldActive := TRUE; start/restart timer.\n    Execute TON with IN := _HoldActive. But if timer already IN=true, changing? TON only restarts if IN false. Could manually set _HoldTimer(IN := FALSE) then call? In one scan, calling TON twice could issue first false reset then true start. But maybe model. Alternative implement own LTIME hold elapsed. Add `_HoldElapsed : LTIME`; on rising set 0; each valid call, if hold active? add? Need hold measured since rising independent of invocations and CycleTime? dt_ms governs timers by scan semantics, and CycleTime is input representing elapsed. They say hold timer 500ms; normally should use actual elapsed dt_ms, but FB sees only CycleTime, assumed equal dt_ms on timed tests. First invocation elapsed zero. Could accumulate CycleTime? However definition says CycleTime attributed current invocation, and hold starts elapsed zero. It likely expects use TON with CycleTime? TON uses task cycle, not input CycleTime. Benchmark simulator dt_ms maps to timer maybe. Formal verifier likely models timers? We need use standard TON to let simulator/formal know timing. Need understand verify tool. We can test.\n\nOption use TON with IN holds. Handle rising reset by `TON` call twice:\n```\nIF NOT THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID THEN\n  _HoldActive := FALSE;\n  _HoldTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#500MS);\nELSIF THICKNESS_FAULT AND NOT _PrevValidFault THEN\n  _HoldActive := TRUE;\n  _HoldTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#500MS);\n  _HoldTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#500MS);\n  // elapsed 0 this invocation\nELSE\n  _HoldTimer(IN := _HoldActive, PT := T#500MS);\n  IF _HoldTimer.Q THEN _HoldActive := FALSE; END_IF\nEND_IF\nTHICKNESS_ALARM := valid AND (fault OR _HoldActive);\n```\nBut if rising while timer already running, first false resets, second true starts. Good. But two TON calls in one cycle may weird in CODESYS; no issue maybe second sees false->true and starts. However simulation abstraction may treat TON differently.\n\nCould implement own timer with `_HoldElapsed` LTIME or TIME and add CycleTime when valid and hold active. But first call starts hold elapsed 0, and release at 500ms if fault absent. Need on each invocation add? Semantics hold active (<500ms since rising), likely elapsed advances with dt_ms. Since CycleTime equals dt and positive, can use CycleTime. But if CycleTime not supplied equals default 0? Timed benchmark uses CycleTime. Yet requirement says \"CycleTime actual elapsed interval represented by this invocation\" so all timing should use it logically. However \"internal hold timer\" and \"timers at elapsed zero\" strongly points to TON, whose elapsed is updated based scan dt, not CycleTime? In simulator, perhaps TON modeled from dt_ms; CycleTime separately. They say first invocation starts IEC timers at elapsed zero. That is explicit TON. So use TON.\n\nNeed determine precise hold: At k=0 fault rising, TON elapsed 0, hold true. At dt 499, timer elapsed 499, active. At 500 after rising? Need cumulative. Release at 500ms if fault absent. TON Q becomes true at elapsed >= PT. So in scan at 500, Q true. What alarm? At monotonic time 500ms since trigger, if fault absent, release. P6 hold = Internal hold timer active (<500ms since last valid rising fault edge). At elapsed 500, not hold, so alarm false. We need process Q then alarm. At 499 active. Good.\n\nBut potential TON semantics: when called with IN false resets elapsed to 0. Rising start. Persistent fault no reset. When fault false but hold active, pass IN true, timer runs until Q then `_HoldActive` false. Alarm should then turn off on same observed scan. Good.\n\nInvalid resets hold immediately, even while fault true. Then later valid fault persistent with prior valid false? Assumptions: \"The counted signal is valid AND fault; a valid fault after signal loss is a new occurrence.\" So upon valid after invalid with fault true, rising edge of valid AND fault, reset timer/start hold, count new. Need track `_PrevValidFault`, set false when invalid. use edge current valid & fault vs previous. It says fault transition maybe valid fault signal edges. Atomic rise = valid AND fault AND NOT previous(valid AND fault). So yes.\n\nFault history: on rise, count increment and shift:\nhistory[4]:=history[3]; ... [1]:=[0]; [0]:=timestamp. If increment overflow out of scope. Counter : UINT increment. Could do `Slab_Fault_Counter := Slab_Fault_Counter + 1;`.\nOutput Slab_Fault_History read-only to callers in CODESYS: declared VAR_OUTPUT cannot truly be read-only, but requirement means internal modifications only. We implement output. Could use VAR_OUTPUT and direct. Good.\n\nRuntime: LTIME accepts TIME? `Slab_Runtime_Accumulator := Slab_Runtime_Accumulator + CycleTime;` implicit conversion TIME to LTIME perhaps CODESYS supports. Need compile. Could use `TIME_TO_LTIME(CycleTime)`? Standard conversion perhaps `TIME_TO_LTIME` exists? CODESYS has `TIME_TO_LTIME`? I think LTIME and TIME conversion functions: `TIME_TO_LTIME`, `LTIME_TO_TIME` may exist. Or adding TIME to LTIME maybe allowed because TIME is 32-bit ms and LTIME 64-bit ns, implicit? In CODESYS, TIME and LTIME are different types but LTIME := TIME likely implicit? Might compile. We can test compile.\n\nNeed initial conditions. FB outputs have no explicit initialization; CODESYS initializes BOOL false, UINT 0, LTIME 0, arrays 0. Fine. Maybe explicit initialize? Could in VAR no. We should perhaps set outputs in declaration? Not needed. Formal initial. If internal `_PrevValidFault` false. Good.\n\nPotential issue alarm while fault true but signal invalid: valid condition. So `THICKNESS_ALARM := THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID AND (THICKNESS_FAULT OR _HoldActive);` Good.\n\nPotential issue hold variable update order and edge. Let's design robust.\n\n```\nVAR\n    _PrevValidFault : BOOL;\n    _HoldActive : BOOL;\n    _HoldTimer : TON;\n    _FaultRise : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\n```\nImplementation:\n```\n_FaultRise := THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID AND THICKNESS_FAULT AND NOT _PrevValidFault;\n\nIF NOT THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID THEN\n    _HoldActive := FALSE;\n    _HoldTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#500MS);\n    THICKNESS_ALARM := FALSE;\nELSE\n    IF _FaultRise THEN\n        _HoldActive := TRUE;\n        _HoldTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#500MS);\n        _HoldTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#500MS);\n    ELSE\n        _HoldTimer(IN := _HoldActive, PT := T#500MS);\n        IF _HoldTimer.Q THEN\n            _HoldActive := FALSE;\n        END_IF;\n    END_IF;\n\n    THICKNESS_ALARM := THICKNESS_FAULT OR _HoldActive;\n\n    IF _FaultRise THEN\n       ... record ...\n    END_IF\nEND_IF;\n\nIF NOT (valid and not fault and cutting_run) then preserve. Better:\nIF THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID AND NOT THICKNESS_FAULT AND CUTTING_RUN THEN\n  Slab_Runtime_Accumulator := Slab_Runtime_Accumulator + CycleTime;\nEND_IF;\n\n_PrevValidFault := THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID AND THICKNESS_FAULT;\n```\nBut on rising fault we invoke TON twice. First false maybe on first-ever call resets. second true starts elapsed 0. Q? CODESYS TON when IN true from false executes start and Q false. Fine.\n\nHowever if `_FaultRise` and timer active, we reset timer and then immediately true, so elapsed 0. Good.\n\nBut if valid persistent fault after invalid, `_PrevValidFault` was false, rise true. Good.\n\nIf invalid and fault true, rise set false; reset alarm and timer. `_PrevValidFault` updated to false.\n\nIf invalid and then next valid nonfault: else branch `_HoldTimer(IN := _HoldActive)` false, remains false, alarm false. Good.\n\nIf valid fault rising at time t, alarm true. Next valid fault persistent (not rise), timer IN := true, time advances, Q eventually true then `_HoldActive` false, but alarm stays due fault. That's okay. A fresh rising edge restarts. If later fault falls, hold inactive (because timer >500) then alarm false. Requirement: hold each triggered alarm at least 500ms, persistent fault >500 means when fault disappears after >500, no hold required. Good. If persistent >500 then fresh rising edge impossible without falling; if falls briefly and rises, rise triggers restart.\n\nIf persistent fault starts and after 200ms falls, timer has 200. On fall, holds to 500, release. Good.\n\nPotential issue `_HoldTimer.Q` remains true if elapsed and IN true; on Q set hold false but we don't force timer IN false until next scan. Next scan IN false resets timer. Fine.\n\nNow formal props. Verify tool requires assertion formulas maybe over declared ports, but likely uses supplied task spec by task id? Tool verify signature source and optional assertions. It will know current task. We can call without assertions. But need compile first. Max 3 each? User says \"Maximum 3 total compiler calls... Maximum 3 total simulation calls.\" We need carefully use. We have compile_st, simulate_st, verify_st. Likely each call counts. We can compile candidate, then simulate selected tests, then verify. If simulation failure repair recompile. Need avoid too many. Let's first formulate candidate.\n\nNeed candidate JSON no markdown final. supporting_objects empty. Could helper DUT? no. Declare all in FB. Need all interface exact. Return implementation body alone no declaration. We can call compile_st. Since no shell. Let's provide preamble? User asks only JSON object final, but commentary can be concise? They explicitly \"Return ONLY a JSON object...\" likely final only JSON; tool preambles may violate? We need use tools with commentary. Could just call tools without preamble? Developer says before tool calls send brief preamble. But user says return only JSON maybe refers final. We can provide minimal commentary? Better no prose except tool calls? Yet system says preamble. We can say \"Compiling the candidate...\" but final only JSON. The requested output perhaps strict. I'll do one short preamble then tools.\n\nBut need save candidate.json. Maybe after verified use shell? Read-only. Could perhaps `compile_st` not write. Is there an implicit requirement evaluator looks at final. Maybe we can use shell once? \"shell commands are unnecessary\" but candidate file requires. Tool sandbox read-only means can't. Could maybe use apply_patch but not present. Maybe `shell_command` in workspace can write notwithstanding read root? Let's later try with Set-Content; if fails no harm? It would be a shell command, despite instruction saying unnecessary. But explicit save to candidate.json likely important. Could use a PowerShell command with .NET File.WriteAllText. Sandbox denies. Is there possibility shell_command has managed read-only but approved writes? no approval never. We can attempt once after final. But user says max tools for compile only, shell unneeded. Maybe not penalized. Let's first compile.\n\nNeed perhaps simulate with tests to catch. Need know simulate_st format. Source declaration implementation. Test cases with steps, expected, optional dt_ms. We need infer expected schema. Likely cases list each e.g.\n```\n[\n {\"name\":\"hold_and_release\",\"steps\":[\n   {\"dt_ms\":0,\"inputs\":{...},\"expected\":{...}},\n ...\n ]}\n]\n```\nWe need inspect tool docs? No. Maybe MCP tool accepts free object. We need construct plausible. Could call simulate after compile. Maybe error shows schema. But limited 3 calls; errors count? likely yes. Let's think. Could use formal verify alone plus maybe compile. But user explicitly use simulator. Need do at least one simulation. We can build comprehensive test case in likely schema. Need know expected format from STAR PLC benchmark. Maybe their MCP server expects cases with `name`, `steps` containing `inputs`, `expected`. Let's search? Can't inspect dirs? User says do not inspect other directories. We can inspect current? No shell unnecessary and forbidden other dirs. We can perhaps infer.\n\nNeed compile first. Let's call compile_st."},"sequence_number":1268}

